Are you ready for Fast Fact No.

129
00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:05.170
2?

130
00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:06.310
Yes.

131
00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:07.970
Fast Fact No.

132
00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:08.390
2.

133
00:06:08.390 --> 00:06:13.170
We got a ton of mail, notably from Zach M.

134
00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:15.410
in Venice Center, New York, and Tom C.

135
00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:16.050
from Milwaukee,

136
00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:22.250
Wisconsin, who would like to know, how did you guys get Dan Castellaneta as a guest star?

137
00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:27.910
I grew up watching The Simpsons, so it always makes me smile to get to actually see the voice

138
00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:31.770
actors in another show, especially Dan Castellaneta.

139
00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:36.590
Well, guys, if you didn't know, Dan is the voice of Homer Simpson.

140
00:06:36.590 --> 00:06:43.830
And in this episode, he played Mr. Ramesh, the CEO of Prestige Direct Mail Solutions.

141
00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:50.350
Danny Chun had been a writer on The Simpsons before joining The Office, and Danny said he

142
00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:56.050
couldn't remember if Allison Jones suggested Dan for the role or if Danny Chun had thought

143
00:06:56.050 --> 00:07:02.010
of it, but he said Dan is, quote, literally one of the funniest people in TV history, and

144
00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:03.810
nobody knows it because he's a cartoon.

145
00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:10.090
So I was incredibly excited to get him on camera because he's a really good actor in live action

146
00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:10.830
as well.

147
00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:12.450
He was fantastic.

148
00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:17.530
I imagine Greg was really excited about this as well, since Greg had written some Simpsons

149
00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:18.170
episodes.

150
00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:18.950
Yeah.

151
00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:23.390
And we had, remember, we had the Homer doll on set the whole time.

152
00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:28.810
Yeah, we got some fan mail from people asking if Dan knew about that or if he saw it, but

153
00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:30.610
he never came to our stages.

154
00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:31.150
That's true.

155
00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:32.350
We didn't get to meet him.

156
00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:37.090
I'll get to it, but all of the Prestige stuff was shot on location.

157
00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:38.810
Oh, yeah, that's right.

158
00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:39.790
All right.

159
00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:41.510
Moving right along, Fast Fact No.

160
00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:45.390
3 is another guest star in this episode, Chris Bauer as

161
00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:46.650
Harry Janarone.

162
00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:51.270
You might recognize Chris from The Wire, Third Watch, and True Blood.

163
00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:57.210
He has also appeared in Law & Order three different times, playing three different people.

164
00:07:57.390 --> 00:07:58.870
That's the Law & Order way.

165
00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:00.530
They bring you back.

166
00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:01.850
It's like a soap opera.

167
00:08:02.390 --> 00:08:03.610
Weren't you the mailman?

168
00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:05.010
No, now I'm a detective.

169
00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:09.030
Well, in 1992, he played Homeless David.

170
00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:12.030
In 1993, he played Mickey Scott.

171
00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:15.150
And by 2002, he made his way up to Sergeant.

172
00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:16.410
There you go.

173
00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:18.890
So he went from Homeless David to Sergeant.

174
00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:19.630
Good job.

175
00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:24.850
He's also been in Law & Order Criminal Intent and Law & Order Special Victims Unit.

176
00:08:25.670 --> 00:08:28.470
He's really mostly done dramas, not comedies.

177
00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:33.350
And Danny Chun said casting Chris Bauer is an example of something they did a lot.

178
00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:36.930
They would cast actors from dramas that the writers loved.

179
00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:39.510
And The Wire was a writer favorite.

180
00:08:39.770 --> 00:08:41.570
That's how they thought of Chris for this role.

181
00:08:41.670 --> 00:08:43.190
He was from The Wire season two.

182
00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:48.490
Danny said the actors from dramas would always come to set and be really nervous on the first

183
00:08:48.490 --> 00:08:51.270
day and they would say things like, I'm just, I'm not funny.

184
00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:52.990
I don't know what to do.

185
00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:56.490
And that we would just say, play it straight and you will be funny.

186
00:08:57.050 --> 00:08:59.030
And of course, he was.

187
00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:00.490
He's so good in this episode.

188
00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:05.710
And then, you know, we also talked to Warren and Halstead about this episode and they said

189
00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:08.230
that Chris was one of their favorite guest actors ever.

190
00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:11.870
And they added what a great all-around guy he was to work with.

191
00:09:11.870 --> 00:09:16.890
And I was so excited when he was cast because I was watching True Blood at the time and I

192
00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:17.950
was like, oh my God.

193
00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:19.570
Oh, that's right.

194
00:09:19.570 --> 00:09:21.210
You had a big True Blood phase.

195
00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:22.190
I remember this.

196
00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:25.670
And I wanted like all the details, but I didn't bug him.

197
00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:27.030
You should have bugged him.

198
00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:27.850
I know.

199
00:09:28.070 --> 00:09:34.450
Now this version of me, 52-year-old me would be like, okay, let's talk for a second.

200
00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:36.350
I'm going to be 50 next year.

201
00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:38.030
And I really feel that, Angela.

202
00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:43.290
I feel like 50 is an opening of a sort of floodgate.

203
00:09:43.590 --> 00:09:44.170
Mm-hmm.

204
00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:44.830
It is.

205
00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:45.090
Yeah.

206
00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:50.670
Of confidence and not giving a s*** about a lot of things that used to make me anxious.

207
00:09:51.150 --> 00:09:51.370
Yeah.

208
00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:53.390
I don't fix the back of my hair anymore.

209
00:09:53.570 --> 00:09:56.050
That's what you can do in your 50s because you don't give a s***.

210
00:09:56.330 --> 00:09:57.530
It's so freeing.

211
00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:00.350
I'm really excited to cross this line.

212
00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:00.870
Yeah.

213
00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:02.790
And I would say this also.

214
00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:09.150
Also, there's something about the 50s for me that is just a world of new curiosities.

215
00:10:09.430 --> 00:10:15.010
I get very curious about things that I think in the past I was like too busy to look at,

216
00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:16.930
you know, too busy to give time to.

217
00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:19.010
And now I'm like, hey, you know what?

218
00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:19.550
I don't know.

219
00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:21.610
I'm halfway to the end here.

220
00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:24.310
Maybe I'll start looking up more stuff.

221
00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:25.130
Yeah.

222
00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:31.010
Maybe I'll learn all of the different varieties of roses and when they bloom best and in what

223
00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:31.530
condition.

224
00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:32.150
Sure.

225
00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:33.770
Now you know that.

226
00:10:34.110 --> 00:10:34.430
Mm-hmm.

227
00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:36.710
You didn't care about that when you were 26.

228
00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:37.850
I didn't.

229
00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:39.130
Or all my hummingbirds.

230
00:10:39.410 --> 00:10:43.970
I mean, the 50s are very, it's a wild time, you guys.

231
00:10:44.990 --> 00:10:47.190
Well, that's all I have for fast facts.

232
00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:48.510
Let's take a break.

233
