Things are really bad under Robert California, I guess.

504
00:24:09.240 --> 00:24:10.820
It's like a festival of poo.

505
00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:13.840
Jim says, hey, hey, come on, language.

506
00:24:14.260 --> 00:24:17.560
Dwight says, yeah, and we're not interested in your sour grapes, okay?

507
00:24:18.060 --> 00:24:20.220
Jim, tell him where he can stick his grapes.

508
00:24:20.380 --> 00:24:22.020
And Jim goes, in the fridge.

509
00:24:22.600 --> 00:24:23.800
And Dwight's like, no.

510
00:24:23.800 --> 00:24:25.460
No, no, Jim, the butt.

511
00:24:25.640 --> 00:24:26.200
It's in the butt.

512
00:24:26.500 --> 00:24:31.380
But Jim, he can't focus on zingers right now because there's too many clients up for grabs.

513
00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:34.600
Lady, I'm going to tell you that Kimber P.

514
00:24:34.820 --> 00:24:37.840
from Virginia Beach is so happy that you just read that dialogue

515
00:24:37.840 --> 00:24:42.680
because Kimber wrote in to say, tell him where he can put his sour grapes, Jim.

516
00:24:42.980 --> 00:24:43.800
In the fridge.

517
00:24:44.140 --> 00:24:47.640
This is my favorite line in the entire series.

518
00:24:47.960 --> 00:24:50.820
My husband and I quote this day in and day out.

519
00:24:50.960 --> 00:24:51.860
It's that good.

520
00:24:51.860 --> 00:24:53.980
In the fridge.

521
00:24:54.380 --> 00:24:57.120
I also just love Festival of Poo.

522
00:24:57.440 --> 00:25:01.880
There's some very clever dialogue in this episode overall, I have to say.

523
00:25:02.340 --> 00:25:06.340
For example, Robert is now going to enter.

524
00:25:06.580 --> 00:25:12.300
He shushes everyone, and then he vomits violently into Jim's trash can.

525
00:25:12.560 --> 00:25:13.360
Oh, yeah.

526
00:25:13.360 --> 00:25:14.760
It's super gross.

527
00:25:15.160 --> 00:25:17.400
We had a fan mail flurry about it.

528
00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:24.900
But Caleb in Omaha, Nebraska, and many others wrote, I have waited so long to send this in.

529
00:25:25.260 --> 00:25:31.600
In season six, episode four, Niagara, the cold open shows Pam vomiting just a little tiny bit,

530
00:25:31.600 --> 00:25:35.020
and then everyone loses it, and everyone's vomiting everywhere.

531
00:25:35.020 --> 00:25:41.200
But in this episode, Robert full-on gross vomits, and everyone acts like nothing happened.

532
00:25:41.520 --> 00:25:42.920
I hear you, Caleb.

533
00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:47.920
In addition to no one vomiting, no one even acknowledges it.

534
00:25:48.180 --> 00:25:51.420
After he vomits, Oscar just says, why did Binghamton close?

535
00:25:52.000 --> 00:25:53.000
What's that about?

536
00:25:53.320 --> 00:25:54.100
You know what?

537
00:25:54.100 --> 00:25:55.880
I was curious about this scene, too.

538
00:25:56.020 --> 00:25:57.360
I have a few things to point out.

539
00:25:57.520 --> 00:26:00.200
First of all, Angela Martin is grossed out.

540
00:26:00.340 --> 00:26:02.280
She has a face of, like, ew, gross.

541
00:26:02.700 --> 00:26:02.900
True.

542
00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:04.520
She doesn't get sick.

543
00:26:04.820 --> 00:26:09.380
I also want you to know, I found in my digital clutter, that I had had a costume fitting,

544
00:26:09.380 --> 00:26:12.520
and that was a new outfit Angela Martin is wearing.

545
00:26:12.920 --> 00:26:16.060
So just a new snazzy Angela Martin outfit.

546
00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:19.020
But yes, I was very curious about this scene, too.

547
00:26:19.160 --> 00:26:21.840
Oscar's reaction didn't seem like it came in the right place.

548
00:26:21.840 --> 00:26:24.660
We also got a question about this scene from Peter F.

549
00:26:24.720 --> 00:26:25.400
in New Hampshire.

550
00:26:25.920 --> 00:26:30.600
Peter wanted to know if in the shooting draft, was it scripted for someone to ask if Robert

551
00:26:30.600 --> 00:26:35.740
was okay, and that line was just cut for time, or if this was an apparent lack of care of

552
00:26:35.740 --> 00:26:39.040
his well-being, which made me laugh.

553
00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:42.220
Okay, so Peter, was it scripted?

554
00:26:42.320 --> 00:26:43.080
Let me tell you.

555
00:26:43.540 --> 00:26:45.900
Here's what I found out when I went to the shooting draft.

556
00:26:46.420 --> 00:26:49.720
All of those lines were there, but they were in a different order because the scene was

557
00:26:49.720 --> 00:26:50.020
bigger.

558
00:26:50.020 --> 00:26:53.340
So it would have actually made more sense.

559
00:26:53.560 --> 00:26:55.300
This is how it read in the shooting draft.

560
00:26:55.700 --> 00:26:58.180
Robert comes in, and he says,

561
00:26:58.700 --> 00:27:03.760
If the content of your cries have no merit, please adjust the volume accordingly.

562
00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:06.580
Pam puts down her phone.

563
00:27:06.840 --> 00:27:10.080
Pam says, Sounds like somebody had a wild night.

564
00:27:10.500 --> 00:27:13.380
Robert panics and throws up into a trash can.

565
00:27:13.660 --> 00:27:17.060
Robert says, Oh God, Pam, quit shrieking.

566
00:27:17.060 --> 00:27:20.940
Oscar says, Robert, can you tell us why Binghamton closed?

567
00:27:21.260 --> 00:27:23.340
Robert puts his hand to his head with pain.

568
00:27:23.540 --> 00:27:24.800
Can everyone just please?

569
00:27:24.960 --> 00:27:26.940
I had a one-man Saturnalia last night.

570
00:27:27.080 --> 00:27:32.180
I celebrated the finalization of my divorce by getting into a case of Australian reds, and

571
00:27:32.180 --> 00:27:34.080
how should I say this, Colombian whites.

572
00:27:34.400 --> 00:27:36.080
It was an outrageous night.

573
00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:38.360
Meredith says, Sure was.

574
00:27:38.480 --> 00:27:39.620
You and me did it.

575
00:27:39.620 --> 00:27:43.000
Robert looks at her wondering.

576
00:27:43.380 --> 00:27:46.240
He picks a reaction and then says, Ha ha.

577
00:27:46.800 --> 00:27:49.240
Then says, Okay, what is this about Binghamton?

578
00:27:49.680 --> 00:27:52.040
Kevin said, The branch closed forever.

579
00:27:52.540 --> 00:27:54.440
Oscar told me it went to heaven.

580
00:27:54.960 --> 00:27:58.240
Then I said, I'm not sure that makes sense, Oscar.

581
00:27:58.380 --> 00:27:59.620
I'm not sure it does.

582
00:27:59.980 --> 00:28:04.080
Then Angela would have said, You knew about Binghamton, right?

583
00:28:04.810 --> 00:28:07.280
And Robert would say, Angela, I'm the CEO.

584
00:28:07.480 --> 00:28:08.840
I know everything.

585
00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:12.700
And Kevin, corporations can't go to heaven because they aren't people.

586
00:28:13.240 --> 00:28:15.480
So that was the full scene.

587
00:28:16.140 --> 00:28:20.980
Oscar's question still does come a little abruptly, but there were more lines before it.

588
00:28:21.660 --> 00:28:22.200
Interesting.

589
00:28:22.500 --> 00:28:23.440
Very interesting.

590
00:28:23.820 --> 00:28:26.620
Was anyone else curious what a Saturnalia was?

591
00:28:26.720 --> 00:28:27.660
Because I didn't know what it was.

592
00:28:27.780 --> 00:28:28.520
Did you know what it was?

593
00:28:28.940 --> 00:28:31.940
I mean, I deduced what it was, I guess.

594
00:28:32.080 --> 00:28:33.200
A big old party?

595
00:28:33.200 --> 00:28:35.840
Yeah, but I was like, why is it called that?

596
00:28:36.420 --> 00:28:37.080
So lady.

597
00:28:38.560 --> 00:28:41.300
I'm like, I'm such an old lady that Googles.

598
00:28:41.460 --> 00:28:42.700
I'm like, so lady.

599
00:28:43.200 --> 00:28:44.240
Lay it on me.

600
00:28:44.440 --> 00:28:46.040
Look what I found when I Googled.

601
00:28:46.240 --> 00:28:50.840
It's an ancient Roman festival and holiday in honor of the god Saturn.

602
00:28:51.320 --> 00:28:54.740
It was held on December 17th in the Julian calendar.

603
00:28:55.040 --> 00:28:59.340
And later, the festivities expanded all the way until December 23rd.

604
00:28:59.460 --> 00:29:02.900
The holiday was celebrated with a sacrifice at the Temple of Saturn.

605
00:29:03.200 --> 00:29:08.940
And then a public banquet followed by private gift giving and lots and lots of partying.

606
00:29:09.220 --> 00:29:13.780
A very carnival atmosphere that overturned even Roman social norMs.

607
00:29:13.780 --> 00:29:20.680
And it said they had a gift exchange that were usually gag gifts, or small figurines made of wax or pottery.

608
00:29:21.080 --> 00:29:25.120
Which kind of sounds like it was a huge party and then a little Yankee swap.

609
00:29:25.860 --> 00:29:27.060
That's what I thought.

610
00:29:27.060 --> 00:29:33.440
It sounds a little bit like a Yankee swap holiday party slash...

611
00:29:33.440 --> 00:29:36.480
Slash on the level that the Romans could party.

612
00:29:37.120 --> 00:29:37.360
Right.

613
00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:40.960
And then plus a sacrifice of some kind at the beginning.

614
00:29:41.240 --> 00:29:42.120
Well, there's that.

615
00:29:42.420 --> 00:29:42.840
Wow.

616
00:29:43.220 --> 00:29:44.060
But there you go.

617
00:29:44.180 --> 00:29:46.060
That's what Robert had all by himself.

618
00:29:46.360 --> 00:29:47.380
Those Romans.

619
00:29:47.760 --> 00:29:51.200
They really, really lived a life, didn't they?

620
00:29:52.160 --> 00:29:53.820
You're so ready to be 50.

621
00:29:53.820 --> 00:29:55.000
I just want you to know.

622
00:29:55.460 --> 00:29:56.100
Is that?

623
00:29:56.220 --> 00:29:57.260
I am, right?

624
00:29:57.820 --> 00:29:59.760
You don't say that in your 20s.

625
00:29:59.920 --> 00:30:02.280
That is nothing that comes out of your mouth in your 20s.

626
00:30:02.280 --> 00:30:02.860
I'll tell you what.

627
00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:03.720
I'll tell you what.

628
00:30:03.840 --> 00:30:04.520
Those Romans.

629
00:30:04.820 --> 00:30:07.900
They lived a life, the Romans did.

630
00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:08.800
They knew how to plan a party.

631
00:30:09.320 --> 00:30:14.080
Something we haven't mentioned in all of this is that Phyllis and Stanley warn everybody,

632
00:30:14.080 --> 00:30:19.420
hey, you're going after these Binghamton clients, but I don't think the Syracuse branch is going

633
00:30:19.420 --> 00:30:24.500
to like this very much because they're going to say, these are New York clients.

634
00:30:24.860 --> 00:30:25.780
Stay out of New York.

635
00:30:26.040 --> 00:30:29.720
We got a very passionate fan question from Rob L.

636
00:30:29.720 --> 00:30:31.820
in Syracuse, New York about this.

637
00:30:32.440 --> 00:30:36.540
Rob said, where the hell has the Syracuse branch been all these years?

638
00:30:36.840 --> 00:30:38.340
It's never been mentioned once.

639
00:30:38.680 --> 00:30:39.380
Not once.

640
00:30:39.820 --> 00:30:42.440
It is not represented in Company Picnic.

641
00:30:42.440 --> 00:30:47.080
And don't get me started on the decision to give Buffalo orange shirts, all caps.

642
00:30:47.520 --> 00:30:49.200
Seriously, who made that call?

643
00:30:49.400 --> 00:30:50.480
Rob would like to know.

644
00:30:50.980 --> 00:30:56.160
Rob goes on to say, frankly, to us Syracuse fans, the exclusion of Syracuse up to this

645
00:30:56.160 --> 00:30:57.600
point has made a little sense.

646
00:30:57.900 --> 00:31:01.200
In New York, we are one of the Thruway Four.

647
00:31:01.480 --> 00:31:06.080
That is the biggest cities along the Thruway, which from west to east are as follows.

648
00:31:06.280 --> 00:31:10.620
Buffalo, Rochester, Syracuse, in all caps, and Albany.

649
00:31:10.620 --> 00:31:13.800
All of which had branches except Syracuse.

650
00:31:14.020 --> 00:31:16.380
Oh, but yet there was a Utica branch?

651
00:31:16.740 --> 00:31:17.700
Four question marks?

652
00:31:18.480 --> 00:31:24.260
Little Utica, which is almost like one of our suburbs, had a branch and representation, and

653
00:31:24.260 --> 00:31:27.000
yet Syracuse didn't exist until this episode.

654
00:31:27.220 --> 00:31:27.800
Come on.

655
00:31:28.460 --> 00:31:30.760
Then Rob says, sorry for my angry typing.

656
00:31:30.960 --> 00:31:35.080
It's just something that's bothered Syracuse office fans for years, and I think we deserve

657
00:31:35.080 --> 00:31:35.860
some answers.

658
00:31:35.860 --> 00:31:36.900
P.S.

659
00:31:36.980 --> 00:31:39.760
Jen and Angela, we are not angry with you for this snub.

660
00:31:40.040 --> 00:31:44.360
Please know that you are still cordially invited to an amazing dinner at Postabilities when

661
00:31:44.360 --> 00:31:45.700
you come to town, my treat.

662
00:31:47.220 --> 00:31:50.060
I want to go to Postabilities so bad.

663
00:31:50.300 --> 00:31:51.700
Rob, I loved your letter.

664
00:31:51.920 --> 00:31:52.780
I really loved it.

665
00:31:52.880 --> 00:31:56.880
I loved the multiple question marks and the all caps and the multiple parentheses.

666
00:31:57.700 --> 00:31:58.940
It stole my heart.

667
00:31:59.200 --> 00:32:01.740
Thank you for your letter, your angry letter about Syracuse.

668
00:32:02.280 --> 00:32:04.780
Yes, Rob, I was right there with you.

669
00:32:04.960 --> 00:32:09.520
I actually wrote in my document, if you could see it, Rob, I put Syracuse branch?

670
00:32:09.700 --> 00:32:11.900
Two question marks, one exclamation point.

671
00:32:12.280 --> 00:32:13.780
Oh, you and Rob are friends.

672
00:32:13.960 --> 00:32:14.800
We're friends.

673
00:32:15.020 --> 00:32:19.440
So I got to digging because I was curious, what are all the Dunder Mifflin branches that

674
00:32:19.440 --> 00:32:20.900
have ever been mentioned on the show?

675
00:32:21.220 --> 00:32:22.280
Here's what I found.

676
00:32:22.440 --> 00:32:24.560
If I missed one, please let me know.

677
00:32:24.560 --> 00:32:32.480
So, okay, we have Akron, Albany, Nashua, New York, which was the corporate office, Rochester,

678
00:32:32.920 --> 00:32:39.140
Utica, Binghamton, Buffalo, Camden, Pittsfield, Stanford, Yonkers, and of course, Scranton.

679
00:32:39.820 --> 00:32:45.040
And then I think there was, was there a time when they were going to combine Scranton and

680
00:32:45.040 --> 00:32:47.220
Stanford and call it the Northeastern branch?

681
00:32:47.620 --> 00:32:49.820
I kind of vaguely remember that as well.

682
00:32:49.980 --> 00:32:51.620
But did I miss any?

683
00:32:51.820 --> 00:32:52.880
I think those are all of them.

684
00:32:52.880 --> 00:32:54.800
Um, I don't even know.

685
00:32:55.400 --> 00:32:56.680
It sounded good.

686
00:32:57.060 --> 00:32:57.580
Okay.

687
00:32:58.480 --> 00:33:00.440
Well, Rob, thank you for writing in.

688
00:33:00.540 --> 00:33:01.780
I was thrown as well.

689
00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:06.700
You know, Angela, Rob was not the only person from Syracuse that wrote us about this episode.

690
00:33:06.840 --> 00:33:09.080
We also got mail from Kim M.

691
00:33:09.320 --> 00:33:13.240
in Syracuse, New York, who said, quote, as someone who was

692
00:33:13.240 --> 00:33:17.520
born and raised in Syracuse, we are very peculiar about its pronunciation.

693
00:33:18.060 --> 00:33:18.420
Oh.

694
00:33:18.420 --> 00:33:24.900
Most people I've met in the area pronounce it Syracuse, which is the same way that Harry

695
00:33:24.900 --> 00:33:30.820
Jennerone from Syracuse pronounces it at six minutes and one second and 15 minutes and 33

696
00:33:30.820 --> 00:33:31.400
seconds.

697
00:33:31.400 --> 00:33:36.820
But one of the biggest indicators that someone is not a local is when they use the pronunciation

698
00:33:37.380 --> 00:33:42.180
Syracuse, which is what Dwight says at 16 minutes and nine seconds.

699
00:33:42.880 --> 00:33:49.780
And then Phyllis at three minutes and four seconds calls it Syracuse, which is accepted by locals.

700
00:33:50.600 --> 00:33:56.640
Kim just wanted to say she really appreciated this tiny detail in pronunciation throughout the episode

701
00:33:56.640 --> 00:33:59.800
and also said props to everyone for saying Binghamton correctly.

702
00:34:00.160 --> 00:34:00.700
Oh.

703
00:34:01.100 --> 00:34:03.320
I say Syracuse, like Phyllis.

704
00:34:03.720 --> 00:34:06.360
I guess people from St. Louis say Syracuse.

705
00:34:07.420 --> 00:34:07.960
Syracuse.

706
00:34:08.160 --> 00:34:10.360
But Syracuse is correct.

707
00:34:10.760 --> 00:34:10.980
Okay.

708
00:34:11.060 --> 00:34:12.460
Like the name Sarah, Syracuse.

709
00:34:12.520 --> 00:34:12.980
Syracuse.

710
00:34:13.180 --> 00:34:13.700
Noted.

711
00:34:13.980 --> 00:34:14.500
Thank you, Kim.

712
00:34:15.140 --> 00:34:17.240
Well, Robert is now going to have a talking head.

713
00:34:17.380 --> 00:34:22.060
He says closing the Binghamton branch never occurred to him before, or I guess last night,

714
00:34:22.060 --> 00:34:24.160
but in vino veritas, as they say.

715
00:34:24.160 --> 00:34:26.820
So I'm not going to start doubting my drunken self now.

716
00:34:27.120 --> 00:34:30.720
I didn't know what in vino veritas meant, so I looked it up.

717
00:34:31.080 --> 00:34:32.060
Did you know?

718
00:34:32.520 --> 00:34:33.860
I had an idea.

719
00:34:34.160 --> 00:34:37.280
It probably meant with wine comes truth or something like that.

720
00:34:37.480 --> 00:34:38.360
That is it.

721
00:34:38.560 --> 00:34:39.280
Oh, really?

722
00:34:39.880 --> 00:34:40.420
I guessed.

723
00:34:41.140 --> 00:34:41.700
Yeah.

724
00:34:41.880 --> 00:34:44.280
It's Latin for in wine there is truth.

725
00:34:44.720 --> 00:34:46.800
Oh, I was so close with my guess.

726
00:34:46.800 --> 00:34:53.940
And there's also an extended saying, in vino veritas, in aqua sanitas, which means in wine

727
00:34:53.940 --> 00:34:56.360
there is truth, in water there is good sense.

728
00:34:56.800 --> 00:34:58.240
Mm, that's smart.

729
00:34:58.500 --> 00:34:58.740
Yeah.

730
00:34:59.120 --> 00:34:59.340
Yeah.

731
00:34:59.480 --> 00:34:59.800
That's true.

732
00:35:00.160 --> 00:35:01.100
It is true.

733
00:35:02.240 --> 00:35:07.420
Speaking of water, Robert is chugging water in the kitchen when Nellie approaches him.

734
00:35:07.900 --> 00:35:15.080
She wants him to know that she got his voicemail last night, and her answer is yes, yes, yes,

735
00:35:15.560 --> 00:35:17.360
yes, and never.

736
00:35:17.660 --> 00:35:21.720
I thought the way Catherine Tate delivered that was so rich.

737
00:35:22.100 --> 00:35:27.400
Like, I don't know what she was thinking of, but you saw her brain go through it, and

738
00:35:27.400 --> 00:35:31.160
I just thought it was such a great moment the way she delivered it.

739
00:35:31.380 --> 00:35:32.680
The specificity.

740
00:35:32.940 --> 00:35:33.400
Yeah.

741
00:35:33.580 --> 00:35:34.040
Made it very funny.

742
00:35:34.040 --> 00:35:35.260
It made me really curious.

743
00:35:35.560 --> 00:35:35.720
Yeah.

744
00:35:35.980 --> 00:35:40.540
We had a fan question from Tally S in Austin, Texas, who wanted to know if there were any

745
00:35:40.540 --> 00:35:45.720
deleted scenes or script notes that tell us exactly what Robert left on Nellie's voicemail.

746
00:35:46.080 --> 00:35:46.600
Ange?

747
00:35:46.940 --> 00:35:47.300
Okay.

748
00:35:47.560 --> 00:35:51.080
Tally, I was super curious, too, and I went through the shooting draft.

749
00:35:51.260 --> 00:35:52.540
I couldn't find it anywhere.

750
00:35:52.780 --> 00:35:54.020
They never share.

751
00:35:54.160 --> 00:35:57.400
It's up to our imagination, which is probably how it should be, right?

752
00:35:58.060 --> 00:35:58.260
Yeah.

753
00:35:58.260 --> 00:36:00.040
So, nothing in the shooting draft.

754
00:36:00.420 --> 00:36:07.900
Well, after Nellie leaves the kitchen, Robert is going to ask Pam, when did you live so intensely

755
00:36:07.900 --> 00:36:10.580
that your brain couldn't hold the memories?

756
00:36:11.780 --> 00:36:16.780
And Pam starts to share, and basically, Robert's just like, I need you to find out what's on

757
00:36:16.780 --> 00:36:17.700
Nellie's voicemail.

758
00:36:17.800 --> 00:36:18.120
Okay?

759
00:36:18.260 --> 00:36:18.500
Yes.

760
00:36:18.500 --> 00:36:20.740
And Pam says, I'm kind of busy.

761
00:36:21.040 --> 00:36:21.500
Yeah.

762
00:36:22.000 --> 00:36:26.500
And I want you to know this scene was longer, and it was very hard to get through.

763
00:36:26.760 --> 00:36:30.820
I did not have a lot of one-on-one scenes with James Spader.

764
00:36:31.020 --> 00:36:32.560
This is one of the only ones.

765
00:36:33.120 --> 00:36:35.420
And I was so delighted to get to do it.

766
00:36:35.540 --> 00:36:38.140
But he is a very intense person.

767
00:36:38.660 --> 00:36:43.140
As an actor, especially, he can zone in on you.

768
00:36:43.140 --> 00:36:49.720
And it was very funny to me, because, you know, Pam says, oh, I just have a lot of things

769
00:36:49.720 --> 00:36:50.240
to do.

770
00:36:50.380 --> 00:36:52.240
And he says, why don't you make a list?

771
00:36:53.080 --> 00:36:55.240
And then in the episode, she just says, you know what?

772
00:36:55.280 --> 00:36:56.120
Why don't I find out?

773
00:36:56.180 --> 00:36:57.440
He's like, yeah, that's great.

774
00:36:57.640 --> 00:37:01.580
But there would have been a little bit more where she starts trying to list things.

775
00:37:02.060 --> 00:37:08.540
She's like, well, I have some voicemails, and I have some emails.

776
00:37:09.220 --> 00:37:13.300
And she's really trying, and he's just staring at her.

777
00:37:14.020 --> 00:37:15.880
And it was so funny to me.

778
00:37:15.960 --> 00:37:16.740
I kept breaking.

779
00:37:17.640 --> 00:37:21.360
Probably that's why it's not in there, because we probably never got a take that was usable.

780
00:37:21.620 --> 00:37:23.400
But anyway.

781
00:37:24.940 --> 00:37:30.820
I would have been so intimidated and just probably forgotten my lines when he locked that intense

782
00:37:30.820 --> 00:37:32.600
gaze like at you, you know?

783
00:37:32.820 --> 00:37:36.500
It was one of my favorite shoot days, because I got him one-on-one.

784
00:37:36.500 --> 00:37:39.160
And he was an actor that I admired so much.

785
00:37:39.340 --> 00:37:39.860
Oh, yeah.

786
00:37:40.180 --> 00:37:45.060
I mean, anytime Angela Martin even had a line exchange with him from across the room, I

787
00:37:45.060 --> 00:37:48.500
was excited that I got to briefly act with him.

788
00:37:49.500 --> 00:37:54.160
Well, up next, a very angry man is about to storm into the office.

789
00:37:54.520 --> 00:38:01.260
But before this scene starts with Harry, there was a little couplet of dialogue that got deleted.

790
00:38:01.260 --> 00:38:02.760
It's in the shooting draft.

791
00:38:03.000 --> 00:38:04.560
Jenna, it just cracked me up.

792
00:38:04.660 --> 00:38:05.460
I wanted to share it.

793
00:38:05.620 --> 00:38:05.740
Okay?

794
00:38:06.260 --> 00:38:06.600
Yes.

795
00:38:07.020 --> 00:38:09.740
It says, Andy has a pan on a hot plate.

796
00:38:09.940 --> 00:38:12.380
He flambes some cherry's jubilee.

797
00:38:12.620 --> 00:38:14.800
A large flame shoots out of the pan.

798
00:38:15.060 --> 00:38:17.440
The camera pushes to Kevin for reaction.

799
00:38:18.180 --> 00:38:21.540
Kevin says, can I have any you drop on the floor?

800
00:38:22.020 --> 00:38:25.180
Andy says, no, Kevin, you can't eat off the floor.

801
00:38:25.760 --> 00:38:30.520
Kevin says, can't or may not, because I have and I'd like to continue.

802
00:38:31.740 --> 00:38:34.870
Isn't that funny?

803
00:38:35.670 --> 00:38:42.210
Well, when Harry storms into the office, he says, who the hell are Jim Halpert and Dwight

804
00:38:42.210 --> 00:38:42.730
Schrute?

805
00:38:43.170 --> 00:38:46.730
And Aaron says, Jim, Dwight, what are your last names?

806
00:38:46.730 --> 00:38:50.470
And then we have this great detail that I just loved.

807
00:38:50.650 --> 00:38:57.790
At five minutes and 55 seconds, Dwight is reaching into his desk drawer and he says, and you are?

808
00:38:57.790 --> 00:39:00.870
For one of his, like, nunchucks or something?

809
00:39:01.070 --> 00:39:01.490
Yes.

810
00:39:02.010 --> 00:39:02.470
Yes.

811
00:39:02.470 --> 00:39:05.410
I had to go to the script to see if this was scripted.

812
00:39:05.430 --> 00:39:06.770
And it was.

813
00:39:07.030 --> 00:39:11.650
And I also found a very fun couplet of dialogue that got cut out.

814
00:39:12.590 --> 00:39:17.150
So after Aaron would have said, Jim, Dwight, what are your last names?

815
00:39:17.430 --> 00:39:20.970
Jim was going to turn to Stanley and say, Jim, run.

816
00:39:21.970 --> 00:39:25.790
And then Stanley would have pointed to Jim and said, that's Halpert.

817
00:39:26.250 --> 00:39:28.290
Pointed to Dwight and said, that's Schrute.

818
00:39:28.290 --> 00:39:31.930
Then he was going to point to Phyllis and say, she's got money.

819
00:39:32.290 --> 00:39:36.230
And then he points to Angela and says, and that one is married to a senator.

820
00:39:36.510 --> 00:39:39.290
And Angela was going to shout, state senator.

821
00:39:41.010 --> 00:39:42.790
And then the scene would have continued.

822
00:39:44.070 --> 00:39:45.690
That was very funny to me.

823
00:39:46.030 --> 00:39:47.110
Yeah, I love it.

824
00:39:47.290 --> 00:39:52.650
And I feel like I remember that moment, Angela, of shooting, of you shouting, state senator.

825
00:39:53.330 --> 00:39:54.710
It's like the one time.

826
00:39:54.890 --> 00:39:56.050
Not that big of a deal.

827
00:39:56.330 --> 00:39:56.650
Exactly.

828
00:39:56.650 --> 00:39:59.310
You don't claim him as a full senator.

829
00:39:59.330 --> 00:39:59.510
Don't come for me.

830
00:39:59.810 --> 00:40:00.310
Exactly.

831
00:40:01.350 --> 00:40:07.110
All the while this is going on, Andy is still making his Cherry's Jubilee right by front reception.

832
00:40:07.830 --> 00:40:08.350
Yes.

833
00:40:08.530 --> 00:40:11.670
And there actually was a giant flame in the scene.

834
00:40:11.890 --> 00:40:16.830
We got a fan question from Victoria Kaye in Wisconsin who said, was the fire real?

835
00:40:17.110 --> 00:40:20.370
And if so, I need details on that safety meeting.

836
00:40:20.930 --> 00:40:23.110
Victoria, yes, the fire was real.

837
00:40:23.110 --> 00:40:29.590
But Steve Burgess says, we did not use booze to make the flame like you would in a traditional Cherry's Jubilee.

838
00:40:30.050 --> 00:40:33.630
Our special effects team wanted a more controlled fire.

839
00:40:33.970 --> 00:40:40.250
So they used something similar to the lighter fluid you might use for a barbecue grill that burns off.

840
00:40:40.370 --> 00:40:41.610
But it was kind of more of a gel.

841
00:40:42.230 --> 00:40:46.450
There was a huge safety meeting with the crew and actors before we shot.

842
00:40:46.650 --> 00:40:56.330
And they also took Ed aside separately and did some training for him so that he didn't hurt himself with the fire and using the gel and all that stuff.

843
00:40:57.110 --> 00:40:59.950
So, yes, there was a very big safety meeting.

844
00:41:00.370 --> 00:41:01.150
Safety meeting.

845
00:41:01.350 --> 00:41:03.410
Open flame by front reception.

846
00:41:03.910 --> 00:41:04.770
That's right.

847
00:41:05.270 --> 00:41:12.230
And, you know, this scene is going to end with Harry saying, I want to talk with Jim and Dwight O and Lloyd Gross right now.

848
00:41:12.730 --> 00:41:13.950
Where's Lloyd Gross?

849
00:41:14.510 --> 00:41:15.850
Who is Lloyd Gross?

850
00:41:16.170 --> 00:41:19.610
Just one of my absolute favorite details of this whole episode.

851
00:41:19.890 --> 00:41:20.510
I know.

852
00:41:20.630 --> 00:41:21.290
You loved Lloyd.

853
00:41:21.550 --> 00:41:22.410
I loved Lloyd.

854
00:41:22.410 --> 00:41:30.710
So Jim and Dwight are going to have a joint talking head, and they're going to share that they figured out a way around the commission cap imposed on salesmen at Dunder Mifflin.

855
00:41:30.970 --> 00:41:35.150
They invented a fake salesman to steal money from the company.

856
00:41:35.510 --> 00:41:37.550
Basically, they're kind of embezzling.

857
00:41:37.790 --> 00:41:40.550
It sounds a little sketchy, but they said it helps them get more money.

858
00:41:40.770 --> 00:41:42.850
And Pam made a drawing of Lloyd.

859
00:41:43.110 --> 00:41:45.250
He's a combination of all the salesmen.

860
00:41:45.490 --> 00:41:47.470
I would like to point out two things here.

861
00:41:47.650 --> 00:41:48.250
What is it?

862
00:41:48.510 --> 00:41:52.050
Number one, Pam once again has drawn someone in the office.

863
00:41:52.410 --> 00:41:57.830
This just further proves my theory that she has a drawer full of sketches of everyone in the office.

864
00:41:58.210 --> 00:41:58.790
So strange.

865
00:41:59.610 --> 00:42:04.010
Number two, Reign's bangs are so long.

866
00:42:04.510 --> 00:42:05.030
Yeah.

867
00:42:05.050 --> 00:42:07.670
They are the longest I have seen them this whole season.

868
00:42:08.130 --> 00:42:09.990
I think he's due for a haircut.

869
00:42:10.230 --> 00:42:14.950
You know, the guy's always got a haircut at the beginning of the season, and we're coming to the end of season eight.

870
00:42:15.230 --> 00:42:16.990
Those bangs are long, folks.

871
00:42:17.230 --> 00:42:18.390
Just wanted to call it out.

872
00:42:18.390 --> 00:42:24.250
I always have to wonder if he was growing them out for some project that was coming up on our summer break.

873
00:42:24.430 --> 00:42:25.770
I'm going to look into it.

874
00:42:25.870 --> 00:42:26.690
Look into it.

875
00:42:26.870 --> 00:42:35.910
I need to say something about this information in the Jim and Dwight talking head where they reveal that they have invented a whole person.

876
00:42:35.910 --> 00:42:36.550
Yeah.

877
00:42:36.710 --> 00:42:38.750
To which they are funneling sales.

878
00:42:39.110 --> 00:42:39.530
Yeah.

879
00:42:40.070 --> 00:42:41.470
To get around a commission cap.

880
00:42:41.670 --> 00:42:44.650
And then I guess they're cashing these checks and splitting the money.

881
00:42:45.150 --> 00:42:46.150
You know what?

882
00:42:47.290 --> 00:42:59.030
When people come for Pam, and you know there's a group of people who come for Pam a lot, one of their big complaints is that she invented a job for herself.

883
00:42:59.030 --> 00:43:01.410
You know, that wasn't reception and that wasn't sales.

884
00:43:01.530 --> 00:43:06.350
She's like this administrative coordinator, and people are just like, she is a liar.

885
00:43:06.530 --> 00:43:07.450
She is a cheat.

886
00:43:07.570 --> 00:43:08.330
I hate her.

887
00:43:08.770 --> 00:43:09.530
I'm sorry.

888
00:43:09.530 --> 00:43:11.690
She is at least working a job.

889
00:43:11.890 --> 00:43:17.630
She comes in every day and works a job, and she contributes to the function of Dunder Mifflin.

890
00:43:17.630 --> 00:43:29.170
And maybe she lied her way into the job, sure, but these same people who come for Pam, I do not see them posting endlessly about this embezzlement scheme from Jim and Dwight.

891
00:43:29.850 --> 00:43:31.070
I wonder why.

892
00:43:31.950 --> 00:43:35.530
Someone's hot to trot over this topic, I can tell.

893
00:43:35.530 --> 00:43:50.990
I am hot to trot over the men who come for Pam with their laundry list of ways that she is not an obedient woman and isn't all working as they want her to,

894
00:43:51.270 --> 00:43:57.010
but then it's crickets when Jim and Dwight steal money from the company.

895
00:43:57.390 --> 00:43:58.050
Mm-hmm.

896
00:43:58.450 --> 00:43:59.070
I'm done.

897
00:43:59.290 --> 00:44:00.070
Do you feel better?

898
00:44:00.690 --> 00:44:01.490
A little.

899
00:44:01.810 --> 00:44:02.330
Okay.

900
00:44:02.650 --> 00:44:03.430
It's the beginning.

901
00:44:03.870 --> 00:44:05.170
We'll wait till season nine.

902
00:44:05.530 --> 00:44:06.670
Oh, my gosh.

903
00:44:06.770 --> 00:44:08.170
I've been thinking about it.

904
00:44:08.770 --> 00:44:15.110
Listen, I think we should take a break because when we come back, I've got some stuff to share about Lloyd Gross.

905
00:44:15.630 --> 00:44:18.790
Yeah, we've got a big conference room meeting, and it's delicious.

906
00:44:19.670 --> 00:44:21.610
We'll be back, and I need to calm down.

907
00:44:33.250 --> 00:44:37.710
Well, we are back, and we are going to find out who the heck Lloyd Gross is.

908
00:44:38.330 --> 00:44:41.130
Dwight points to Toby and says, that's Lloyd.

909
00:44:41.650 --> 00:44:45.170
Well, this is going to lead to a conference room scene, and Harry is upset.

910
00:44:45.170 --> 00:44:52.430
He is saying that Binghamton, those clients should go to Syracuse because that's more in their sort of district or region.

911
00:44:52.810 --> 00:44:55.630
Well, he says they shouldn't be crossing the state lines.

912
00:44:55.850 --> 00:45:02.090
But then Lloyd points out that Scranton is actually closer to Binghamton than Syracuse is.

913
00:45:02.630 --> 00:45:02.870
Mm-hmm.

914
00:45:03.230 --> 00:45:03.390
Yeah.

915
00:45:03.890 --> 00:45:11.250
Toby's going to have a talking head now where he says, I like to think Lloyd Gross is a no-nonsense guy who doesn't back down from anybody.

916
00:45:11.250 --> 00:45:14.590
Well, I loved the storyline of Toby as Lloyd.

917
00:45:15.170 --> 00:45:18.910
I saw in deleted scenes, he even took it one step further.

918
00:45:19.270 --> 00:45:27.090
Toby has a full-length leather coat made with the initials LG monogrammed on the lapel.

919
00:45:27.370 --> 00:45:29.450
I asked Warren and Halstead about this.

920
00:45:29.570 --> 00:45:33.270
They said they don't remember exactly where Lloyd Gross came from.

921
00:45:33.270 --> 00:45:42.710
But since Toby was this closet novelist, they thought it would be funny if he really took the character seriously and tried to give him a backstory.

922
00:45:43.310 --> 00:45:49.570
In their minds, they said Toby getting to take the role of LG was one of the most exciting things to happen to Toby,

923
00:45:49.810 --> 00:45:53.250
probably since getting to be on the jury for the Scranton Strangler.

924
00:45:53.250 --> 00:45:58.990
Well, I have a lot of questions about how and when Toby got this jacket made.

925
00:45:59.210 --> 00:46:04.470
I'm assuming that he just ran with this new character after today.

926
00:46:04.850 --> 00:46:13.730
Or possibly Lloyd, you know, this sort of scam of how they make money has been around for a while, but they're just now sharing it.

927
00:46:14.270 --> 00:46:14.530
Hmm.

928
00:46:15.110 --> 00:46:15.490
True.

929
00:46:15.750 --> 00:46:18.990
He might have had a long time to get that jacket ordered and made.

930
00:46:18.990 --> 00:46:27.750
Well, this conference room gets very heated, but I have to say, Toby is far more confident as Lloyd than he is as Toby.

931
00:46:28.050 --> 00:46:28.750
I know.

932
00:46:28.750 --> 00:46:29.450
I know.

933
00:46:29.530 --> 00:46:30.570
It's so hilarious.

934
00:46:30.930 --> 00:46:33.950
He pretends to get a text from the wife.

935
00:46:34.110 --> 00:46:35.310
He's got to take that.

936
00:46:35.830 --> 00:46:36.050
Yeah.

937
00:46:36.330 --> 00:46:38.410
I love Toby as Lloyd.

938
00:46:40.050 --> 00:46:45.090
I could have used a webisode all about his alter ego, Lloyd.

939
00:46:45.490 --> 00:46:45.810
Yeah.

940
00:46:46.470 --> 00:46:46.890
Mm-hmm.

941
00:46:47.450 --> 00:46:47.730
True.

942
00:46:47.730 --> 00:46:50.290
Like, did he have to go to the DMV?

943
00:46:50.450 --> 00:46:52.470
Does he have a Lloyd Gross driver's license?

944
00:46:52.710 --> 00:46:54.230
Like, how far did this go?

945
00:46:54.730 --> 00:46:55.670
You know what?

946
00:46:56.110 --> 00:46:58.970
There's a lot of things we don't know about Toby, I think.

947
00:46:59.210 --> 00:46:59.770
Oh, yeah.

948
00:47:00.190 --> 00:47:04.270
He might be actually more mysterious than Creed.

949
00:47:04.790 --> 00:47:07.370
You know, because Creed really lays it all out there.

950
00:47:07.530 --> 00:47:10.510
But Toby has some secret identities, I think.

951
00:47:10.630 --> 00:47:10.830
Yeah.

952
00:47:10.990 --> 00:47:12.110
He's got some secrets.

953
00:47:12.650 --> 00:47:12.950
Mm-hmm.

954
00:47:13.450 --> 00:47:17.210
Well, ultimately, in this conference room scene, they're going to decide, you know what?

955
00:47:17.310 --> 00:47:19.370
Let's let Robert settle this.

956
00:47:20.050 --> 00:47:21.810
Harry said he didn't realize Robert was there.

957
00:47:22.430 --> 00:47:22.950
Yeah.

958
00:47:23.210 --> 00:47:26.290
It made me think, like, why only Scranton?

959
00:47:26.410 --> 00:47:31.370
Why does Robert only camp out at Scranton, at the Scranton branch?

960
00:47:31.510 --> 00:47:33.470
Why does he own a home in Scranton?

961
00:47:34.130 --> 00:47:35.850
I'm so baffled by that.

962
00:47:35.850 --> 00:47:39.470
My voice went into seven different octaves to say that sentence.

963
00:47:39.930 --> 00:47:42.590
Why does he have a home in Scranton?

964
00:47:42.750 --> 00:47:46.850
I mean, I would say he likes the Scranton branch because Nellie's there.

965
00:47:47.350 --> 00:47:51.970
But I kind of think Robert probably has someone in every port, right?

966
00:47:52.230 --> 00:47:52.990
The sailor.

967
00:47:53.290 --> 00:47:54.430
What's that phrase?

968
00:47:54.730 --> 00:47:55.490
I don't know.

969
00:47:55.490 --> 00:47:56.730
Is that that song?

970
00:47:56.930 --> 00:47:59.570
Brandy, you're a fine girl.

971
00:47:59.630 --> 00:48:02.910
What a good wife you would be.

972
00:48:03.250 --> 00:48:03.710
No.

973
00:48:03.950 --> 00:48:04.410
No.

974
00:48:08.550 --> 00:48:09.190
Okay.

975
00:48:10.370 --> 00:48:13.030
Nellie is not Robert's Brandy?

976
00:48:13.790 --> 00:48:14.430
Yes.

977
00:48:14.430 --> 00:48:19.790
But I thought you were asking me if there was a song about having a ship in every port.

978
00:48:20.030 --> 00:48:23.390
You know, the sailor has a place to land everywhere.

979
00:48:23.390 --> 00:48:25.610
Lady and every stop of the ship.

980
00:48:26.090 --> 00:48:26.370
Yes.

981
00:48:27.130 --> 00:48:27.550
Right.

982
00:48:28.670 --> 00:48:29.470
Where are we?

983
00:48:29.610 --> 00:48:30.190
I don't know.

984
00:48:30.430 --> 00:48:31.090
We don't know.

985
00:48:31.350 --> 00:48:31.890
Here we go.

986
00:48:32.070 --> 00:48:33.010
Listen, here's where we are.

987
00:48:33.190 --> 00:48:35.450
Andy is at the sink and he's washing dishes.

988
00:48:36.170 --> 00:48:39.630
I don't know why I loved that so much, but it was very funny to me.

989
00:48:39.730 --> 00:48:41.070
I thought Ed was very funny.

990
00:48:41.950 --> 00:48:48.730
And he's going to be caught in the middle of this conversation between Harry and Robert,

991
00:48:48.730 --> 00:48:55.330
where Harry says, listen, Binghamton is closed and I want Prestige Direct Mail Solutions.

992
00:48:55.450 --> 00:48:56.590
They're a big client.

993
00:48:56.990 --> 00:48:59.250
But Dwight says he already put a call into them.

994
00:48:59.470 --> 00:49:03.090
And they say, Robert, you have to decide who gets this client, who gets this client.

995
00:49:03.610 --> 00:49:05.410
This is going to give Andy an idea.

996
00:49:05.570 --> 00:49:08.930
And he is going to run out of the office straight to the elevator.

997
00:49:09.150 --> 00:49:13.430
I have to tell you my favorite line in this scene, though, was when he went to stop washing

998
00:49:13.430 --> 00:49:14.950
the dishes, maybe to get out of the way.

999
00:49:15.050 --> 00:49:17.150
And Harry goes, no, buddy, you finished your dishes.

1000
00:49:17.150 --> 00:49:18.450
I loved it, too.

1001
00:49:19.090 --> 00:49:19.990
Well, you know what?

1002
00:49:20.070 --> 00:49:22.370
Robert is not even going to make a decision.

1003
00:49:22.710 --> 00:49:24.750
He said nobody can have that client.

1004
00:49:24.950 --> 00:49:25.830
He needs a second.

1005
00:49:25.990 --> 00:49:27.030
He has to think about it.

1006
00:49:27.650 --> 00:49:28.550
But you're right.

1007
00:49:28.710 --> 00:49:30.550
Andy is going to make a move.

1008
00:49:30.790 --> 00:49:31.890
They're a huge client.

1009
00:49:32.210 --> 00:49:34.010
He takes off his visitor badge.

1010
00:49:34.170 --> 00:49:34.910
He's off.

1011
00:49:35.430 --> 00:49:35.630
Yep.

1012
00:49:36.310 --> 00:49:37.910
Harry's going to have the same idea.

1013
00:49:38.370 --> 00:49:40.810
He's going to say, you know, Robert's kind of a weirdo.

1014
00:49:40.910 --> 00:49:42.390
I'm going to go outside and get some air.

1015
00:49:43.110 --> 00:49:46.250
That's when Jim realizes, uh-oh, he goes to the window.

1016
00:49:46.250 --> 00:49:48.850
So now Harry's peeling out of the parking lot.

1017
00:49:49.250 --> 00:49:50.790
Dwight and him are like, damn it.

1018
00:49:50.930 --> 00:49:52.890
So now they joined the chase.

1019
00:49:53.270 --> 00:49:56.690
I had to ask Steve Burgess, where did we do these driving scenes?

1020
00:49:57.550 --> 00:50:03.490
And Steve said we did them on Chandler Boulevard, which is very close to our studio.

1021
00:50:03.870 --> 00:50:06.750
He said we started with these scenes on a Friday morning.

1022
00:50:06.910 --> 00:50:09.450
And then after they were done, we moved back to the stages.

1023
00:50:09.450 --> 00:50:14.130
We talked to Danny about doing these driving scenes, about directing them.

1024
00:50:14.230 --> 00:50:18.830
And he said they can always be a bit stressful and the resets take a long time.

1025
00:50:19.110 --> 00:50:25.090
But that our crew was so amazing, they made it easy and allowed him to really focus on the creative.

1026
00:50:25.750 --> 00:50:27.430
And then he did share one thing.

1027
00:50:27.550 --> 00:50:32.750
He said also sometimes as we would be filming these scenes, a random person would drive by,

1028
00:50:32.950 --> 00:50:35.310
look over, and realize it was Jim and Dwight in the car.

1029
00:50:35.310 --> 00:50:36.350
And then they'd freak out.

1030
00:50:37.970 --> 00:50:40.870
I remember that happening when we would do driving scenes.

1031
00:50:41.250 --> 00:50:41.510
Yeah.

1032
00:50:41.730 --> 00:50:46.210
And you can't have people being like, oh my God, like in the scene.

1033
00:50:46.570 --> 00:50:47.030
Totally.

1034
00:50:47.550 --> 00:50:50.270
We had a fan catch from this scene, Ange.

1035
00:50:50.630 --> 00:50:52.070
Oh, I have my own catch.

1036
00:50:52.190 --> 00:50:52.730
What do you got?

1037
00:50:53.170 --> 00:50:54.570
Well, Taylor R.

1038
00:50:54.790 --> 00:50:57.250
from Sioux Falls, South Dakota said,

1039
00:50:57.250 --> 00:51:03.390
When Jim jumps out of his car and opens up Harry Janarone's car door to slow him down,

1040
00:51:03.710 --> 00:51:09.070
the car Harry is driving looks like a 2012 Cadillac CTS-V.

1041
00:51:09.690 --> 00:51:14.890
And its doors should automatically lock when Harry drives over eight miles per hour.

1042
00:51:15.050 --> 00:51:18.850
So unless Harry was driving really slowly, his door should have been locked.

1043
00:51:19.190 --> 00:51:20.830
That's such a good catch.

1044
00:51:21.210 --> 00:51:21.610
Yeah.

1045
00:51:22.010 --> 00:51:23.150
That's a car catch.

1046
00:51:23.390 --> 00:51:25.070
So what was your catch, Angela?

1047
00:51:25.830 --> 00:51:28.950
Well, mine happens at 11 minutes and 30 seconds.

1048
00:51:29.310 --> 00:51:30.890
I'm going to call bullshit here.

1049
00:51:31.150 --> 00:51:32.370
Guys, I'm playing a card.

1050
00:51:32.590 --> 00:51:33.870
I'm leaning forward.

1051
00:51:34.050 --> 00:51:34.510
What is it?

1052
00:51:34.710 --> 00:51:34.970
Okay.

1053
00:51:35.590 --> 00:51:39.970
Jim looks directly to camera and gives one of his Jim quote looks.

1054
00:51:39.970 --> 00:51:41.930
You know, the look that Jim gives to camera.

1055
00:51:42.090 --> 00:51:42.730
We know it.

1056
00:51:43.090 --> 00:51:48.990
Right after he hits the nose, you know, Dwight's like, can you put this into overdrive here?

1057
00:51:49.150 --> 00:51:51.130
He's like, oh yeah, I'll put it in the nose.

1058
00:51:51.410 --> 00:51:53.050
The windshield wipers are going.

1059
00:51:53.050 --> 00:51:58.830
Jim looks right to the camera and gives a look like, I'm sorry.

1060
00:51:59.050 --> 00:52:07.930
Does Jim know that the camera crew is in front of him with like maybe a camera on the dash or the hood or like he's a follow car?

1061
00:52:07.930 --> 00:52:09.930
I thought this broke the fourth wall.

1062
00:52:09.930 --> 00:52:12.030
I don't think it does.

1063
00:52:12.270 --> 00:52:17.170
I think it does beg the question, what is the budget for this documentary film crew?

1064
00:52:17.390 --> 00:52:25.650
And how are they gathering this footage, this sudden car chase race to this client's office?

1065
00:52:25.650 --> 00:52:31.550
They were not expecting to hop in a car and film this sequence today for this documentary.

1066
00:52:31.550 --> 00:52:42.430
So they had to have a crew at Dunder Mifflin Scranton that catches Jim's reaction to Harry pulling out of the parking lot.

1067
00:52:42.630 --> 00:52:45.430
And then they had to have a crew already on the road.

1068
00:52:47.150 --> 00:52:50.830
But I thought Jim looking at camera in this moment kind of broke the fourth wall.

1069
00:52:50.830 --> 00:52:55.030
I don't think it breaks the fourth wall because we know that they are camera aware.

1070
00:52:55.470 --> 00:52:57.650
We know that they know they're being filmed.

1071
00:52:57.870 --> 00:53:03.470
But I think it does make you think too hard about the documentary crew.

1072
00:53:04.050 --> 00:53:04.950
That's what it did.

1073
00:53:05.110 --> 00:53:05.670
Okay, fine.

1074
00:53:05.910 --> 00:53:07.410
It made me think too hard.

1075
00:53:07.410 --> 00:53:14.070
We did get some mail from people that's been stuck in my head where they pointed out,

1076
00:53:14.290 --> 00:53:20.950
how does this documentary still have a budget after all of these eight years that they've been filming?

1077
00:53:20.950 --> 00:53:23.530
Because they haven't aired a single piece of footage.

1078
00:53:24.690 --> 00:53:27.130
Like, who is funding this?

1079
00:53:27.710 --> 00:53:28.350
PBS?

1080
00:53:28.970 --> 00:53:30.310
Yeah, I don't know.

1081
00:53:30.510 --> 00:53:35.610
They are so committed to something happening at this paper company of value.

1082
00:53:36.350 --> 00:53:39.330
There must be an annual meeting where they're like, what do we think?

1083
00:53:39.490 --> 00:53:40.790
Do we keep filming them?

1084
00:53:40.950 --> 00:53:43.350
They're like, yes, but I'm going to need to add to the budget.

1085
00:53:43.350 --> 00:53:47.650
I need to have a flatbed on call in case one of them jumps in their car.

1086
00:53:48.090 --> 00:53:48.530
Yeah.

1087
00:53:48.890 --> 00:53:50.390
Because we're going to want to get that.

1088
00:53:50.970 --> 00:53:52.290
And they're like, that's fine.

1089
00:53:52.290 --> 00:53:54.710
Also, I need a whole bunch of lipstick cameras.

1090
00:53:54.990 --> 00:54:00.290
And I need someone that can install them inside the dash of their vehicles.

1091
00:54:01.030 --> 00:54:01.470
Correct.

1092
00:54:02.090 --> 00:54:02.690
All right.

1093
00:54:02.830 --> 00:54:05.510
Maybe I rescind my bullshit card.

1094
00:54:05.610 --> 00:54:07.430
But it did make me think real hard.

1095
00:54:07.790 --> 00:54:09.410
It was a hard-thinking card.

1096
00:54:09.650 --> 00:54:10.930
It was a hard-thinking card.

1097
00:54:11.270 --> 00:54:13.690
Well, Andy is now going to arrive at Prestige.

1098
00:54:13.690 --> 00:54:16.970
He asked to see the CEO, even though he doesn't have an appointment.

1099
00:54:17.350 --> 00:54:21.630
He definitely doesn't think he's going to be able to see the CEO that easily.

1100
00:54:21.830 --> 00:54:23.910
And the receptionist is like, no problem.

1101
00:54:24.070 --> 00:54:24.790
Not a problem.

1102
00:54:24.950 --> 00:54:26.230
I loved her.

1103
00:54:26.530 --> 00:54:27.550
I loved her.

1104
00:54:27.670 --> 00:54:29.050
She was played by Amy Shin.

1105
00:54:29.050 --> 00:54:34.370
Her credits include Arrested Development, Hello, Ladies, and Just Add Magic.

1106
00:54:34.870 --> 00:54:39.310
Danny said that Amy Shin was a suggestion from Alison Jones, and they just loved her.

1107
00:54:39.670 --> 00:54:45.770
He said she just had this really funny attitude, and she was able to take a role that could have just been played straightforward.

1108
00:54:46.650 --> 00:54:47.850
Somewhat forgettable, maybe.

1109
00:54:48.070 --> 00:54:51.190
She gave it a weird flavor, which really brought the character to life.

1110
00:54:51.370 --> 00:54:54.510
It made you want to know more about this oddball receptioniSt.

1111
00:54:54.910 --> 00:54:55.150
Yes.

1112
00:54:55.630 --> 00:54:59.870
Danny said that they always sparked those kinds of auditions, the ones that really surprised them.

1113
00:54:59.950 --> 00:55:03.970
And he said this was hard to do by the time you got to season eight of a show.

1114
00:55:03.970 --> 00:55:06.050
So he really, really loved her.

1115
00:55:06.270 --> 00:55:07.330
I loved her, too.

1116
00:55:07.530 --> 00:55:08.510
I was curious.

1117
00:55:08.510 --> 00:55:10.090
I wanted to know more about her.

1118
00:55:10.570 --> 00:55:13.750
By the way, she has a nameplate on her desk.

1119
00:55:13.830 --> 00:55:16.670
I could only make out the last name of Fogarty.

1120
00:55:16.910 --> 00:55:18.630
I couldn't see the first name.

1121
00:55:18.830 --> 00:55:20.350
It's not in the shooting draft, either.

1122
00:55:20.510 --> 00:55:26.630
So if anyone else was able to screen grab that nameplate and make out the first name, let us know.

1123
00:55:26.910 --> 00:55:31.830
Well, you know, the CEO is going to walk out of his office, and he's going to be like, yeah, happy to see you right now.

1124
00:55:32.590 --> 00:55:44.430
Danny said that Dan Castellaneta has this same sort of unbothered, kind of blissful energy that the character of Mr. Ramesh has, you know,

1125
00:55:44.530 --> 00:55:47.870
in the way that he's just totally happy to meet with Andy on a whim.

1126
00:55:48.370 --> 00:55:51.110
Danny said that that is Dan's real-life energy.

1127
00:55:51.110 --> 00:55:54.950
He is just the most laid-back, content person in the world.

1128
00:55:55.330 --> 00:55:57.010
I wanted to work at Prestige.

1129
00:55:57.170 --> 00:55:58.570
Everyone there is so chill.

1130
00:55:59.150 --> 00:55:59.710
Same!

1131
00:56:00.510 --> 00:56:03.850
Up next, we have some girl talk in the break room.

1132
00:56:04.070 --> 00:56:05.450
Oh, that's a song.

1133
00:56:05.670 --> 00:56:06.970
That is a song.

1134
00:56:07.130 --> 00:56:08.590
Girl talk in the break room.

1135
00:56:09.090 --> 00:56:13.970
Pam is chatting up Nellie, trying to get her to spill the tea about Robert's voicemails.

1136
00:56:14.210 --> 00:56:18.670
And Nellie is about to tell her when Angela interrupts.

1137
00:56:18.770 --> 00:56:20.610
She comes in the break room saying,

1138
00:56:20.770 --> 00:56:22.050
Hello, my clucking hens!

1139
00:56:22.230 --> 00:56:23.970
Got room for another in the roost?

1140
00:56:24.110 --> 00:56:25.570
Don't worry, I won't lay an egg.

1141
00:56:25.570 --> 00:56:29.590
In the script, it said, Pam looks to Angela with the expression,

1142
00:56:29.990 --> 00:56:30.910
What are you doing?

1143
00:56:31.790 --> 00:56:32.750
Angela smirks.

1144
00:56:33.070 --> 00:56:36.970
And then that's when it would cut to Angela's talking head, where she says,

1145
00:56:37.130 --> 00:56:39.250
Robert sent me to take over if Pam fails.

1146
00:56:39.510 --> 00:56:39.750
If.

1147
00:56:40.550 --> 00:56:43.830
Then, in the shooting draft, it goes back to the break room.

1148
00:56:43.830 --> 00:56:48.110
And, lady, I remember this note that they gave me,

1149
00:56:48.190 --> 00:56:52.890
which was that Angela Martin is terrible at girl talk.

1150
00:56:54.110 --> 00:56:56.250
And it was written like this.

1151
00:56:56.510 --> 00:56:58.910
I have been crunching numbers all day.

1152
00:56:59.150 --> 00:57:00.650
Math is for boys!

1153
00:57:00.930 --> 00:57:02.030
Exclamation point.

1154
00:57:02.490 --> 00:57:06.790
I, period, need, period, girl talk, period.

1155
00:57:07.330 --> 00:57:10.250
So just really clunky and forced.

1156
00:57:10.370 --> 00:57:12.810
I loved coming in and saying all of that.

1157
00:57:12.810 --> 00:57:15.490
And then, of course, Gabe overhears and says,

1158
00:57:15.610 --> 00:57:16.910
Did someone say girl talk?

1159
00:57:17.210 --> 00:57:20.650
And then, you know, Gabe has a talking head that comes right after that.

1160
00:57:21.050 --> 00:57:23.810
Well, I think Zach did some improv here.

1161
00:57:23.890 --> 00:57:26.750
Because it was scripted in the shooting draft,

1162
00:57:26.790 --> 00:57:29.050
and there were no candy bag alts for it,

1163
00:57:29.370 --> 00:57:30.370
that Gabe said,

1164
00:57:30.910 --> 00:57:33.010
I am great at girl talk, period.

1165
00:57:33.490 --> 00:57:35.610
But it aired with Gabe saying,

1166
00:57:35.890 --> 00:57:38.250
Sometimes I wonder if I have ovaries in my scrotum,

1167
00:57:38.330 --> 00:57:39.870
because I'm great at girl talk.

1168
00:57:40.070 --> 00:57:42.150
That sounds like Zach to me.

1169
00:57:42.150 --> 00:57:43.330
It really does.

1170
00:57:43.670 --> 00:57:43.890
Yes.

1171
00:57:44.170 --> 00:57:45.170
That's my theory,

1172
00:57:45.330 --> 00:57:46.990
is that he improvised the top part.

1173
00:57:47.810 --> 00:57:49.230
My question is,

1174
00:57:49.410 --> 00:57:52.770
did he improvise anything about Korean soap operas?

1175
00:57:53.170 --> 00:57:54.950
No, that was all scripted.

1176
00:57:55.190 --> 00:57:55.710
Wow.

1177
00:57:56.150 --> 00:57:56.370
Yeah.

1178
00:57:56.790 --> 00:58:00.050
Well, none of this girl talk is helping Pam get to the bottom of things.

1179
00:58:00.270 --> 00:58:00.590
No.

1180
00:58:00.690 --> 00:58:02.330
It's been completely derailed.

1181
00:58:02.830 --> 00:58:06.190
This girl talk in the break room scene actually was extended.

1182
00:58:06.390 --> 00:58:07.990
It had a whole second beat.

1183
00:58:08.210 --> 00:58:09.210
It's in the shooting draft.

1184
00:58:09.210 --> 00:58:11.070
It's also in deleted scenes.

1185
00:58:11.930 --> 00:58:13.690
Daryl is going to join the party.

1186
00:58:13.870 --> 00:58:15.570
But it's not why you think.

1187
00:58:15.570 --> 00:58:19.310
We find out that he has a little friend that lives in the break room.

1188
00:58:19.550 --> 00:58:20.750
I want you to hear it.

1189
00:58:20.750 --> 00:58:25.430
I keep asking myself, what is my skinny girl margarita?

1190
00:58:26.190 --> 00:58:31.410
I think we should all go around in a circle and each tell one secret, except for Gabe.

1191
00:58:31.970 --> 00:58:33.050
Let's go around here.

1192
00:58:34.510 --> 00:58:36.370
What, is everyone just hanging out?

1193
00:58:36.550 --> 00:58:38.330
No, we're letting it all hang out.

1194
00:58:38.970 --> 00:58:40.270
Nellie was going to tell a story.

1195
00:58:40.270 --> 00:58:41.770
Oh, great.

1196
00:58:41.910 --> 00:58:42.510
Sounds good.

1197
00:58:43.170 --> 00:58:44.390
Break room's busy today.

1198
00:58:45.570 --> 00:58:46.450
Nobody knows.

1199
00:58:46.710 --> 00:58:49.190
There's a mouse who lives in the wall behind the vending machine.

1200
00:58:49.570 --> 00:58:50.470
I feed him.

1201
00:58:50.650 --> 00:58:52.550
Now he comes out every day at the same time.

1202
00:58:53.050 --> 00:58:55.130
If people find Squeaker, he's toaSt.

1203
00:58:55.510 --> 00:58:57.050
I just want to give him his food.

1204
00:58:57.830 --> 00:58:59.570
And try one more time with the top hat.

1205
00:59:01.570 --> 00:59:04.970
He's trying to put a top hat on a little mouse named Squeaker?

1206
00:59:05.330 --> 00:59:07.750
Yes, and he holds up the top hat.

1207
00:59:07.910 --> 00:59:12.250
And you know, Craig Robinson has big hands and he holds up a tiny top hat.

1208
00:59:12.250 --> 00:59:13.350
It's so cute.

1209
00:59:13.430 --> 00:59:15.430
A tiny top hat for a little mouse.

1210
00:59:15.570 --> 00:59:15.990
Yes.

1211
00:59:16.190 --> 00:59:20.930
And then there was a whole other scene where we find out that Erin has also been feeding

1212
00:59:20.930 --> 00:59:22.790
a mouse in the break room.

1213
00:59:23.110 --> 00:59:24.610
She doesn't call it Squeaker.

1214
00:59:24.770 --> 00:59:29.590
She says it's like multiple generations in a family she's been feeding for a while.

1215
00:59:29.590 --> 00:59:32.930
And then Daryl's like, how many of these mice are there?

1216
00:59:33.010 --> 00:59:33.990
And she's like, oh, hundreds.

1217
00:59:34.330 --> 00:59:35.770
And now Daryl's like, oh, no.

1218
00:59:36.030 --> 00:59:36.470
Oh, no.

1219
00:59:36.730 --> 00:59:37.550
Pump the brakes.

1220
00:59:37.590 --> 00:59:38.450
What's happening?

1221
00:59:38.830 --> 00:59:40.630
But I did love this callback.

1222
00:59:40.790 --> 00:59:45.970
I pointed it out to Warren and Halstead that Daryl has a soft spot for little creatures.

1223
00:59:46.010 --> 00:59:49.350
Remember in Fun Run where he's feeding the squirrel in the parking lot?

1224
00:59:49.750 --> 00:59:50.270
Oh, yeah.

1225
00:59:50.630 --> 00:59:50.930
Yeah.

1226
00:59:51.510 --> 00:59:55.670
Well, speaking of creatures, we're heading...

1227
00:59:55.670 --> 00:59:56.150
What is this segue?

1228
00:59:56.150 --> 01:00:01.710
Yeah, well, we're heading back to Prestige because Andy is speaking to Mr. Romish.

1229
01:00:01.890 --> 01:00:02.470
Oh, yeah.

1230
01:00:02.750 --> 01:00:08.190
And I realized I have not done a location breakdown of Prestige Direct Mail Solutions.

1231
01:00:08.810 --> 01:00:11.690
How does a creature come into this location breakdown?

1232
01:00:12.050 --> 01:00:13.070
I will tell you.

1233
01:00:13.310 --> 01:00:17.750
Dani shared that they did a big location scout for this episode because they wanted to find

1234
01:00:17.750 --> 01:00:25.090
one location that could be used for the interior offices of Prestige, but also for the lobby

1235
01:00:25.090 --> 01:00:28.830
and elevator scenes and the exterior scenes, including the coffee cart.

1236
01:00:28.950 --> 01:00:30.070
All of that is coming up.

1237
01:00:30.330 --> 01:00:33.110
He said they had to look at a few different office buildings.

1238
01:00:33.110 --> 01:00:40.950
One of them was a vacant office in the valley, but in the middle of the lobby, there was this

1239
01:00:40.950 --> 01:00:45.850
big sort of seemingly abandoned fish tank in there.

1240
01:00:46.030 --> 01:00:49.970
It was really super cloudy and the glass had algae on it.

1241
01:00:50.570 --> 01:00:54.430
But inside the tank, there was an arowana fish.

1242
01:00:55.190 --> 01:01:00.190
I guess it's a pretty large fish that lives in the Amazon.

1243
01:01:01.270 --> 01:01:06.210
And Dani said it was just in there and it was alone.

1244
01:01:06.690 --> 01:01:08.370
I feel bad for it.

1245
01:01:08.470 --> 01:01:09.090
I know.

1246
01:01:09.390 --> 01:01:13.350
You know, Dani wondered, like, who was caring for this?

1247
01:01:13.890 --> 01:01:15.790
Was anyone caring for it?

1248
01:01:16.090 --> 01:01:18.190
Ultimately, they didn't pick that location.

1249
01:01:19.410 --> 01:01:26.710
Incidentally, the United States banned the ownership of arowanas because they're an endangered species.

1250
01:01:27.390 --> 01:01:29.710
Yeah, I was going to say, is that even legal?

1251
01:01:30.310 --> 01:01:32.130
I don't think so.

1252
01:01:32.430 --> 01:01:34.770
What does an arowana look like?

1253
01:01:35.210 --> 01:01:36.990
They're very big.

1254
01:01:37.510 --> 01:01:39.450
Okay, big, check.

1255
01:01:39.450 --> 01:01:47.310
They're sort of, some of them, when I looked at pictures, they have like, they can have

1256
01:01:47.310 --> 01:01:49.930
sort of like an orangey glow.

1257
01:01:50.130 --> 01:01:52.390
Others are kind of a yellowy glow.

1258
01:01:53.370 --> 01:01:54.850
God, what do they look like?

1259
01:01:54.930 --> 01:01:57.010
They have sort of like a mouth that.

1260
01:01:57.430 --> 01:01:59.050
Does it come up from under?

1261
01:01:59.250 --> 01:01:59.910
It comes up.

1262
01:01:59.910 --> 01:02:00.850
Is that why you're doing that?

1263
01:02:01.610 --> 01:02:02.130
Yes.

1264
01:02:02.670 --> 01:02:04.550
It comes up from under.

1265
01:02:05.170 --> 01:02:07.450
Like a big old mouth from under.

1266
01:02:07.450 --> 01:02:12.470
I couldn't see your face just in that one moment because I was Googling what they look like.

1267
01:02:12.630 --> 01:02:16.750
But I could tell from your character what you were doing.

1268
01:02:17.190 --> 01:02:17.450
Yeah.

1269
01:02:17.890 --> 01:02:21.270
And then their eyes look kind of.

1270
01:02:21.870 --> 01:02:24.770
Okay, listen, we don't have to describe this fish anymore.

1271
01:02:25.030 --> 01:02:25.890
We can let it go.

1272
01:02:26.070 --> 01:02:28.090
But we'll put a picture in stories.

1273
01:02:28.510 --> 01:02:28.950
Sure.

1274
01:02:29.190 --> 01:02:32.790
So anyway, that was my segue about strange creatures.

1275
01:02:32.790 --> 01:02:37.870
Steve Burgess told me that ultimately we filmed at the Borax building in Valencia.

1276
01:02:38.150 --> 01:02:39.230
Minus the fish.

1277
01:02:39.590 --> 01:02:41.370
Yeah, there was no fish at this location.

1278
01:02:41.530 --> 01:02:47.550
And they spent all day shooting at this location, which, Angela, means we had a day off.

1279
01:02:48.130 --> 01:02:48.570
Hope.

1280
01:02:49.590 --> 01:02:53.830
Andy's about to win a big old client for his new company, Big Red.

1281
01:02:53.830 --> 01:02:58.210
Instead, he tells Mr. Ramesh that Prestige will get 100% of his attention.

1282
01:02:58.750 --> 01:03:01.710
He can save him 25% on his costs.

1283
01:03:01.970 --> 01:03:05.430
He's going to give him his personal phone number and a key to his house.

1284
01:03:05.710 --> 01:03:06.190
Yeah.

1285
01:03:06.510 --> 01:03:09.110
And his internet password, which is Eat, Pray, Love.

1286
01:03:09.490 --> 01:03:13.830
Where is Andy getting this paper from that he's now going to give to him?

1287
01:03:13.830 --> 01:03:15.270
He's going to figure that out later.

1288
01:03:15.470 --> 01:03:17.410
He's faking it till he can make it.

1289
01:03:17.650 --> 01:03:17.950
Right.

1290
01:03:17.950 --> 01:03:20.090
But he wins over Mr. Ramesh.

1291
01:03:20.450 --> 01:03:24.190
Well, meanwhile, Jim and Dwight arrive at the Prestige headquarters.

1292
01:03:24.650 --> 01:03:26.050
They run in.

1293
01:03:26.210 --> 01:03:28.290
They try to lock the door.

1294
01:03:28.550 --> 01:03:30.310
Yeah, they don't want Harry to come in.

1295
01:03:30.490 --> 01:03:31.750
Dwight takes off his belt.

1296
01:03:32.030 --> 01:03:38.130
You know, he's thinking in the moment and kind of makes some kind of knot ties the door shut.

1297
01:03:38.130 --> 01:03:39.870
But now his pants won't stay up.

1298
01:03:40.030 --> 01:03:45.390
I made a mental note of this moment because I thought if I'm ever being chased

1299
01:03:45.390 --> 01:03:48.970
or maybe during the zombie apocalypse that I prepped for all the time.

1300
01:03:49.230 --> 01:03:49.570
Sure, sure.

1301
01:03:49.930 --> 01:03:52.530
That the belt around the door, it was smart.

1302
01:03:52.790 --> 01:03:56.250
I mean, I know that they can smash the glass and get the belt off,

1303
01:03:56.350 --> 01:03:57.950
but it's definitely going to slow them down.

1304
01:03:58.150 --> 01:04:01.790
But then the big problem I had was I don't ever wear a belt.

1305
01:04:02.210 --> 01:04:03.590
Yeah, well, there's the problem.

1306
01:04:03.850 --> 01:04:07.430
And it made me wonder if I should be wearing.

1307
01:04:07.430 --> 01:04:07.950
Take your bra off.

1308
01:04:07.950 --> 01:04:08.650
Oh, oh.

1309
01:04:08.650 --> 01:04:09.910
Take your bra off.

1310
01:04:09.970 --> 01:04:10.390
Yes.

1311
01:04:10.730 --> 01:04:13.450
Tie that sucker around there a few times.

1312
01:04:13.730 --> 01:04:14.150
Yes.

1313
01:04:14.650 --> 01:04:16.830
When am I going to see that in a zombie movie?

1314
01:04:17.050 --> 01:04:17.770
That's brilliant.

1315
01:04:18.130 --> 01:04:18.930
And you know what?

1316
01:04:19.230 --> 01:04:22.590
Gals, we can take that off so fast through the armpit of your shirt.

1317
01:04:22.790 --> 01:04:23.450
We've all done it.

1318
01:04:23.590 --> 01:04:25.210
Don't even have to take your shirt off.

1319
01:04:25.470 --> 01:04:25.870
No.

1320
01:04:26.190 --> 01:04:26.670
Mm-mm.

1321
01:04:27.190 --> 01:04:28.250
That's so smart.

1322
01:04:29.470 --> 01:04:32.170
Someone, please file that away as a plot point.

1323
01:04:32.170 --> 01:04:32.290
I'm ready.

1324
01:04:32.570 --> 01:04:32.890
I'm ready.

1325
01:04:33.110 --> 01:04:35.250
In a future film or for your real life.

1326
01:04:35.690 --> 01:04:38.110
So anyway, Dwight's pants are falling down.

1327
01:04:38.110 --> 01:04:39.590
He runs into the elevator.

1328
01:04:40.130 --> 01:04:41.270
But guess what?

1329
01:04:41.270 --> 01:04:46.490
But clearly there are other doors to this building because Harry runs into the elevator with them.

1330
01:04:47.070 --> 01:04:50.670
But as the doors are closing, Dwight presses a different floor.

1331
01:04:51.170 --> 01:04:52.850
He forces Jim out.

1332
01:04:52.990 --> 01:04:54.310
He's like, meet me upstairs.

1333
01:04:54.510 --> 01:04:55.190
Run upstairs.

1334
01:04:56.990 --> 01:04:57.910
Jim's like, what?

1335
01:04:58.130 --> 01:04:59.330
And he's like, take the stairs.

1336
01:04:59.490 --> 01:05:01.650
And Jim's like, I don't even know where the stairs are.

1337
01:05:01.650 --> 01:05:08.570
And Dwight starts jumping up and down as hard as he can in the elevator to, what, maybe make it not go?

1338
01:05:08.890 --> 01:05:12.170
He wants to activate the seismic fail safe.

1339
01:05:12.510 --> 01:05:13.250
There it is.

1340
01:05:13.650 --> 01:05:19.270
Well, we asked our director, Danny, which scenes from this episode were special to him, and this was one of them.

1341
01:05:19.370 --> 01:05:20.370
Here's what he said.

1342
01:05:20.370 --> 01:05:25.210
The sequence where they are racing up to the prestige office was the most fun to shoot.

1343
01:05:25.390 --> 01:05:28.670
It was as close as the office gets to an action sequence.

1344
01:05:28.950 --> 01:05:31.350
Rain and John crushed it as always.

1345
01:05:31.710 --> 01:05:44.370
The stuff with Dwight tying the door shut with his belt and then having his pants sag then fully fall down while trying to trigger the elevator fail safe was just sublime physical comedy that totally served the story.

1346
01:05:44.370 --> 01:05:57.570
Oh, and he said, also, I remembered BJ's one piece of directing advice was to have one shot that was ambitious or special, and this scene was mine, and it was so much fun.

1347
01:05:58.270 --> 01:06:02.530
Yeah, Warren and Halstead also shared about this elevator scene.

1348
01:06:02.910 --> 01:06:08.430
You know, Warren pointed out that the camera goes into the elevator, and then it stays with Dwight and Harry.

1349
01:06:08.790 --> 01:06:10.250
Yeah, it's in the corner.

1350
01:06:10.250 --> 01:06:14.290
Warren said this was one continuous shot in a real elevator.

1351
01:06:14.470 --> 01:06:24.370
There was no fakie elevator here that John really did have to run up a flight of stairs in order to be at the elevator doors when they opened again.

1352
01:06:24.370 --> 01:06:26.290
There was no special timing to that.

1353
01:06:26.730 --> 01:06:27.010
Oh, my gosh.

1354
01:06:27.210 --> 01:06:30.310
He said the stairs were not close either.

1355
01:06:30.630 --> 01:06:36.510
He said John had to sprint and run up a flight of stairs numerous times.

1356
01:06:36.510 --> 01:06:44.370
But Warren said John never complained, but all that sweat and him being out of breath, he said that was real.

1357
01:06:44.890 --> 01:06:46.450
It seemed very real.

1358
01:06:46.610 --> 01:06:50.190
When Warren and Halstead shared that with us, I was like, oh, my gosh, I had no idea.

1359
01:06:50.410 --> 01:06:51.250
This is the thing.

1360
01:06:51.330 --> 01:06:59.430
When you don't work on the same day as your fellow actors, I had no idea this whole time that John was running up a flight of stairs for every take.

1361
01:06:59.650 --> 01:07:00.150
I know.

1362
01:07:00.150 --> 01:07:09.390
Well, Dwight, Jim, and Harry are all going to stumble into the prestige offices, and they're going to find out it's too late.

1363
01:07:09.730 --> 01:07:13.250
Mr. Ramesh has already signed with Big Red Paper Company.

1364
01:07:13.510 --> 01:07:17.290
We got a really fun piece of fan mail about this scene.

1365
01:07:17.290 --> 01:07:18.830
It's from Stacey B.

1366
01:07:19.090 --> 01:07:20.930
in Wisconsin, who says,

1367
01:07:21.170 --> 01:07:25.750
My 11-year-old daughter has been waiting to submit a fan catch.

1368
01:07:26.070 --> 01:07:32.210
When Jim and Dwight get to the prestige offices, Mr. Ramesh is holding an orange ball.

1369
01:07:32.710 --> 01:07:34.930
But later in the scene, it's blue.

1370
01:07:35.410 --> 01:07:40.750
My 14-year-old son thinks that the ball has two different sides, one of each color.

1371
01:07:40.750 --> 01:07:49.170
Well, Stacey, first of all, big props to your daughter, because I went back and rewatched the scene, and I saw exactly what she saw.

1372
01:07:49.310 --> 01:07:53.030
In one shot, he's holding an orange ball, and then he's holding a blue ball.

1373
01:07:53.790 --> 01:07:56.990
And shout out to your son, who is right.

1374
01:07:57.310 --> 01:08:00.190
It is a two-sided ball.

1375
01:08:00.590 --> 01:08:00.910
It is.

1376
01:08:00.910 --> 01:08:05.330
You can see both sides very briefly at 15 minutes and 19 seconds.

1377
01:08:05.330 --> 01:08:11.410
The side facing Mr. Ramesh is orange, and the side facing Jim and Dwight and Harry is blue.

1378
01:08:11.810 --> 01:08:12.810
Great catch.

1379
01:08:13.030 --> 01:08:14.090
Great catch.

1380
01:08:14.270 --> 01:08:15.910
I would like to throw this out there.

1381
01:08:16.290 --> 01:08:16.770
Yeah?

1382
01:08:17.470 --> 01:08:20.910
Syracuse University, their colors are orange and blue.

1383
01:08:21.550 --> 01:08:22.190
Hey.

1384
01:08:22.630 --> 01:08:23.090
Hey.

1385
01:08:23.910 --> 01:08:29.130
It looked like a ball, but it also looked like maybe a little football.

1386
01:08:29.470 --> 01:08:32.330
It didn't look spherical to me.

1387
01:08:32.330 --> 01:08:35.110
It looked whatever the shape of a football is.

1388
01:08:35.930 --> 01:08:36.590
That shape.

1389
01:08:37.130 --> 01:08:38.310
A football shape.

1390
01:08:38.450 --> 01:08:43.010
It looked sporty to me, and I think it's a little nod to Syracuse University.

1391
01:08:43.290 --> 01:08:44.230
It probably was.

1392
01:08:45.070 --> 01:08:54.730
Back at Dunder Mifflin, Scranton, Pam is going to go in the conference room and tell Robert that she stole Nellie's phone, and they're going to listen to the messages together.

1393
01:08:54.990 --> 01:08:55.470
Yes.

1394
01:08:55.610 --> 01:08:58.990
And they don't paint a great picture of Nellie's life.

1395
01:08:59.130 --> 01:09:00.770
It really starts to affect Pam.

1396
01:09:01.530 --> 01:09:06.030
Yeah, there's a message from her mom who's just trying to cheer her up.

1397
01:09:06.350 --> 01:09:09.690
The credit card company says she's over her limit.

1398
01:09:09.690 --> 01:09:13.850
Her brother asks if her boss is still hitting on her.

1399
01:09:14.130 --> 01:09:21.990
And then there's a call from an adoption agency rejecting her application to adopt a child.

1400
01:09:22.850 --> 01:09:23.830
Pam's heard enough.

1401
01:09:24.030 --> 01:09:25.370
She deletes all the messages.

1402
01:09:25.670 --> 01:09:28.050
Robert's like, ah, you just messed it all up.

1403
01:09:28.310 --> 01:09:28.970
I know.

1404
01:09:29.150 --> 01:09:30.010
Pam's like, ah.

1405
01:09:30.410 --> 01:09:31.470
That was very funny.

1406
01:09:32.110 --> 01:09:33.490
Fan question from Charlotte H.

1407
01:09:33.670 --> 01:09:34.310
in England.

1408
01:09:35.150 --> 01:09:41.190
Charlotte would like to know who plays Nellie's mom and brother on her voicemail, and are they actually British?

1409
01:09:41.590 --> 01:09:50.070
Well, Charlotte, Nellie's mom's voice was played by our stand-in Lori Sox, who is not British, but who did a very good job on this phone call.

1410
01:09:50.190 --> 01:09:54.570
And I wasn't able to figure out whose voices were on the rest of the messages.

1411
01:09:54.770 --> 01:09:59.290
Steve Burgess said we hired voice performers, but he didn't have any details on who they were anymore.

1412
01:09:59.290 --> 01:10:09.710
Yeah, and I asked Danny about the voice of the brother because I saw an online theory that it was Mackenzie Crook, who played Gareth in the BBC version of The Office.

1413
01:10:10.090 --> 01:10:12.810
But Danny said, no, he didn't think so.

1414
01:10:12.890 --> 01:10:16.530
He said if that was the case, they feel like they would have made it more of a thing.

1415
01:10:16.830 --> 01:10:17.630
For sure.

1416
01:10:17.870 --> 01:10:19.590
He debunked that theory.

1417
01:10:20.010 --> 01:10:23.650
Well, Pam is now going to go into Nellie's office and return her cell phone.

1418
01:10:24.210 --> 01:10:25.770
And Nellie is shopping.

1419
01:10:26.070 --> 01:10:27.310
She's looking at some sandals.

1420
01:10:28.350 --> 01:10:30.250
She wants to buy Pam a pair.

1421
01:10:31.490 --> 01:10:33.530
And they have this really sweet moment.

1422
01:10:34.430 --> 01:10:34.630
Yeah.

1423
01:10:35.070 --> 01:10:38.470
Nellie sort of seems like she's going to finally share the details of this message.

1424
01:10:38.570 --> 01:10:40.090
And Pam is almost stopping her.

1425
01:10:40.230 --> 01:10:41.130
Like, I don't want to know.

1426
01:10:41.310 --> 01:10:42.750
You don't have to talk about it.

1427
01:10:43.010 --> 01:10:45.910
And they do end up bonding sort of sweetly.

1428
01:10:46.530 --> 01:10:50.170
And Nellie has a talking head where she says things are really looking up.

1429
01:10:50.470 --> 01:10:52.490
You know, she might be a mother soon.

1430
01:10:52.490 --> 01:10:56.030
And she has MasterCard just where she wants them.

1431
01:10:56.150 --> 01:10:58.350
And now she has a new friend at work.

1432
01:10:58.870 --> 01:11:06.590
You know, Warren and Halstead said that being able to listen to Nellie's messages gave them an opportunity to add layers to her character.

1433
01:11:06.590 --> 01:11:14.810
They said it was always really fun to explore what characters were like outside of the office, what their aspirations were, what their life was about.

1434
01:11:15.150 --> 01:11:21.210
And learning about Nellie's desire to adopt a baby, it really grounded her, gave her some depth.

1435
01:11:21.390 --> 01:11:25.510
And at this point, we knew that Catherine Tate would be returning next year.

1436
01:11:25.510 --> 01:11:30.070
And they were planting seeds for her storyline in Season 9.

1437
01:11:30.770 --> 01:11:30.970
Yeah.

1438
01:11:31.290 --> 01:11:33.350
I like seeing this side of Nellie.

1439
01:11:33.490 --> 01:11:34.810
I feel like I needed to see it.

1440
01:11:35.470 --> 01:11:36.550
Yeah, me too.

1441
01:11:36.870 --> 01:11:44.290
And she's so good when she has these moments, when she talked about her magician ex-boyfriend.

1442
01:11:44.730 --> 01:11:44.910
Yeah.

1443
01:11:45.310 --> 01:11:47.390
Like, Catherine Tate can do it all.

1444
01:11:47.930 --> 01:11:48.250
Mm-hmm.

1445
01:11:49.050 --> 01:11:55.290
Erin is now going to tell Robert that he has a phone call, someone named Salvation, no last name.

1446
01:11:56.110 --> 01:11:58.050
Robert answers, and it's Andy.

1447
01:11:58.330 --> 01:12:00.030
He has called to taunt him.

1448
01:12:00.670 --> 01:12:10.230
Andy says, hey, the biggest loser in the office just signed the biggest client, so you can either hire me back and get prestigious business back, or he'll find another buyer.

1449
01:12:10.830 --> 01:12:13.070
Oh, this does not sit well with Robert.

1450
01:12:13.830 --> 01:12:15.710
Robert does not care for this.

1451
01:12:16.110 --> 01:12:17.730
He goes on the offensive.

1452
01:12:18.050 --> 01:12:23.230
He says if Andy wants a street fight, he has no idea how ugly it can get.

1453
01:12:23.680 --> 01:12:25.610
He says, you don't even know my real name.

1454
01:12:25.790 --> 01:12:27.310
I'm the f***ing Lizard King.

1455
01:12:28.090 --> 01:12:28.630
What?

1456
01:12:29.130 --> 01:12:29.710
Oh, boy.

1457
01:12:30.710 --> 01:12:31.250
Whoa.

1458
01:12:31.830 --> 01:12:33.710
This was a fan mail flurry, lady.

1459
01:12:34.170 --> 01:12:34.790
Anna W.

1460
01:12:35.070 --> 01:12:38.230
from South Carolina said, I've been waiting to ask this question.

1461
01:12:38.770 --> 01:12:44.370
I'm 32 years old, and everywhere I see people quoting this line, I'm the f***ing Lizard King.

1462
01:12:44.650 --> 01:12:45.810
I don't get it.

1463
01:12:45.930 --> 01:12:47.230
I don't understand it.

1464
01:12:47.530 --> 01:12:48.350
Please help.

1465
01:12:48.750 --> 01:12:49.530
Gabe S.

1466
01:12:49.750 --> 01:12:55.470
from San Francisco said, this is one of the most underrated office quotes, but one of my favorites.

1467
01:12:55.770 --> 01:12:58.670
Please give more details on how this quote came to be.

1468
01:12:58.670 --> 01:13:00.110
And Andrea B.

1469
01:13:00.370 --> 01:13:07.930
from Hamilton, Ontario, Canada said, do you know why the writers had Robert California call himself the f***ing Lizard King?

1470
01:13:08.250 --> 01:13:15.170
Is this a reference to the Doors song, I am the Lizard King, or to Jim Morrison's same nickname?

1471
01:13:16.190 --> 01:13:25.200
Well, everyone, we asked Warren and Halstead about this, and Angela, this was just a small amount of the mail that we got on this subject.

1472
01:13:26.250 --> 01:13:26.570
Oh, yeah.

1473
01:13:27.290 --> 01:13:32.190
Warren and Halstead said, there was no deep thinking about this in the writers' room.

1474
01:13:32.190 --> 01:13:35.070
It was just a room pitch that made everyone laugh.

1475
01:13:35.470 --> 01:13:39.530
You know, sometimes the writers would do what's called a punch-up pass on a script.

1476
01:13:39.530 --> 01:13:48.910
It's where they sit around the conference room table, and they read through the script all together as a group, and people can just shout out extra jokes as they go along.

1477
01:13:49.370 --> 01:13:56.150
And so this was just a shout-out room pitch, and everybody laughed at this idea, and it made it in.

1478
01:13:56.510 --> 01:14:01.730
Well, to all the folks that wrote in about this line, I was super curious about it, too.

1479
01:14:01.730 --> 01:14:08.810
I was wondering, as well, if maybe Robert California was referencing the song Celebration of the Lizard.

1480
01:14:09.090 --> 01:14:12.510
The lyrics were by Jim Morrison, the music by The Doors.

1481
01:14:12.510 --> 01:14:14.810
